    {"q": "Who ordinarily signs pleadings on behalf of the Attorney-General for India or the State Advocate-General?", "opts": ["Any unregistered person attending Court", "Only the Court Master", "An advocate-on-record", "M"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The answer is An advocate-on-record. Pleadings set out each side's case in writing. Their job is to identify the facts the parties rely on and the dispute the Court must decide. Evidence is used later to prove those facts. The provision assigns this specific responsibility to the named person or body. Check the role against the exact action being asked about.", "source": "Order XXIX Rule 6. Source: Gazette p. 38"},
    {"q": "When may the Court strike out or amend objectionable matter in a pleading?", "opts": ["Only after the final decree is executed", "Only before the first summons is issued", "At any stage of the proceeding", "M"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The answer is At any stage of the proceeding. Pleadings set out each side's case in writing. Their job is to identify the facts the parties rely on and the dispute the Court must decide. Evidence is used later to prove those facts. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXIX Rule 7. Source: Gazette p. 38"},
    {"q": "Which description is expressly a ground for striking out matter from a pleading?", "opts": ["A relevant and concise material fact", "A properly pleaded cause of action", "Unnecessary or scandalous matter", "M"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The answer is Unnecessary or scandalous matter. Pleadings set out each side's case in writing. Their job is to identify the facts the parties rely on and the dispute the Court must decide. Evidence is used later to prove those facts. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXIX Rule 7. Source: Gazette p. 38"},
    {"q": "For what principal purpose may an amendment of pleadings be permitted?", "opts": ["Replacing the entire Court administration", "Adding wholly unrelated criminal charges", "D", "Determining the real question in controversy"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The answer is Determining the real question in controversy. Pleadings set out each side's case in writing. Their job is to identify the facts the parties rely on and the dispute the Court must decide. Evidence is used later to prove those facts. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXIX Rule 8. Source: Gazette p. 38"},
    {"q": "If an order granting amendment specifies no time, within how many days must the party amend unless time is extended?", "opts": ["Seven days", "Twenty-eight days", "M", "Fourteen days"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The answer is Fourteen days. Pleadings set out each side's case in writing. Their job is to identify the facts the parties rely on and the dispute the Court must decide. Evidence is used later to prove those facts. The condition in the question determines when this answer applies and should be read along with the main rule.", "source": "Order XXIX Rule 9. Source: Gazette p. 38"},
    {"q": "Who may order an amendment solely to rectify a clerical error without notice?", "opts": ["The Attorney-General personally", "The respondent’s private clerk", "M", "The Registrar"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The answer is The Registrar. Pleadings set out each side's case in writing. Their job is to identify the facts the parties rely on and the dispute the Court must decide. Evidence is used later to prove those facts. The provision assigns this specific responsibility to the named person or body. Check the role against the exact action being asked about.", "source": "Order XXIX Rule 10. Source: Gazette p. 38"},
    {"q": "Unless otherwise ordered, who must receive a copy of an order for a clerical amendment?", "opts": ["Only the Court’s security officer", "Only the initial filing clerk", "D", "All other parties"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The answer is All other parties. Pleadings set out each side's case in writing. Their job is to identify the facts the parties rely on and the dispute the Court must decide. Evidence is used later to prove those facts. The condition in the question determines when this answer applies and should be read along with the main rule.", "source": "Order XXIX Rule 10. Source: Gazette p. 38"},
    {"q": "Which rules of Code Order XI are expressly excepted from application?", "opts": ["Rules 5 and 23", "Rules 4 and 22", "Rules 6 and 21", "Rules 7 and 24"], "ans": 0, "exp": "The answer is Rules 5 and 23. Discovery and inspection help parties find out what relevant material exists and see it before the case is heard. Asking written questions and inspecting or obtaining a document are different ways of gathering that information. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXX Rule 1. Source: Gazette p. 38"},
    {"q": "Who is to answer interrogatories permitted by an order of the Court?", "opts": ["Only the party named as plaintiff", "Persons directed by the Court", "Only the advocate-on-record", "The Registrar personally"], "ans": 1, "exp": "The answer is Persons directed by the Court. Discovery and inspection help parties find out what relevant material exists and see it before the case is heard. Asking written questions and inspecting or obtaining a document are different ways of gathering that information. The provision assigns this specific responsibility to the named person or body. Check the role against the exact action being asked about.", "source": "Order XXX Rule 2. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "At what stage may a defendant apply for leave to deliver interrogatories?", "opts": ["Before entering appearance", "Immediately on receipt of summons", "After filing the written statement", "Only after judgment"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The answer is After filing the written statement. Discovery and inspection help parties find out what relevant material exists and see it before the case is heard. Asking written questions and inspecting or obtaining a document are different ways of gathering that information. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXX Rule 3. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "What must accompany an order made for delivery of interrogatories when served?", "opts": ["A certified copy of the final decree", "A list of all witnesses examined", "A statement of the opposite party’s costs", "One set of interrogatories as allowed"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The answer is One set of interrogatories as allowed. Discovery and inspection help parties find out what relevant material exists and see it before the case is heard. Asking written questions and inspecting or obtaining a document are different ways of gathering that information. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXX Rule 4. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "For sufficient reason, whose affidavit may the Court allow in discovery, production or inspection?", "opts": ["Any person competent to swear it on behalf of the party concerned", "Only the Chief Justice of India", "Only the opposing litigant", "Only the Court-appointed interpreter"], "ans": 0, "exp": "The answer is Any person competent to swear it on behalf of the party concerned. Discovery and inspection help parties find out what relevant material exists and see it before the case is heard. Asking written questions and inspecting or obtaining a document are different ways of gathering that information.", "source": "Order XXX Rule 5. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "What is to be left in the Registry when an ordered document is deposited?", "opts": ["Only the deposition of the first witness", "A copy of the order and a schedule of the document", "The decree and a statement of costs", "A certified copy of every pleading"], "ans": 1, "exp": "The answer is A copy of the order and a schedule of the document. Discovery and inspection help parties find out what relevant material exists and see it before the case is heard. Asking written questions and inspecting or obtaining a document are different ways of gathering that information. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXX Rule 6. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "During a pending suit, when may a depositor obtain return of documents whose purpose is satisfied?", "opts": ["On an oral request without any permission", "Only after the final decree is executed", "With the other party’s written consent or an order of the Court", "Only with the Taxing Officer’s written approval"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The answer is With the other party’s written consent or an order of the Court. Discovery and inspection help parties find out what relevant material exists and see it before the case is heard. Asking written questions and inspecting or obtaining a document are different ways of gathering that information.", "source": "Order XXX Rule 7. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "Which Code Order concerning admissions applies in Supreme Court original suits?", "opts": ["Order XIII of the First Schedule to the Code", "Order XVII of the First Schedule to the Code", "Order XXIII of the First Schedule to the Code", "Order XII of the First Schedule to the Code"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The answer is Order XII of the First Schedule to the Code. An admission is a formal acceptance of a fact or document in a lawsuit. It can narrow what still needs to be proved. This part identifies the procedure used to deal with admissions in the Court. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXI. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "Which sections of the Code are applied to summons to give evidence or produce documents?", "opts": ["Sections 28 and 32", "Sections 25 and 31", "Sections 27 and 33", "Sections 29 and 34"], "ans": 0, "exp": "The answer is Sections 28 and 32. Witness rules explain how someone is called to give evidence or produce a document. A summons for a witness serves a different purpose from the summons that tells a defendant to answer the lawsuit. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXII Rule 1. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "Which Code Order generally governs summoning and attendance of witnesses, subject to exceptions?", "opts": ["Order XIV of the First Schedule", "Order XVI of the First Schedule", "Order XVIII of the First Schedule", "Order XX of the First Schedule"], "ans": 1, "exp": "The answer is Order XVI of the First Schedule. Witness rules explain how someone is called to give evidence or produce a document. A summons for a witness serves a different purpose from the summons that tells a defendant to answer the lawsuit. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXII Rule 2. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "Which Code Order governs adjournments in Supreme Court original suits, subject to a wording substitution?", "opts": ["Order XIX of the First Schedule", "Order XXI of the First Schedule", "Order XVII of the First Schedule", "Order XXIII of the First Schedule"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The answer is Order XVII of the First Schedule. An adjournment puts off a hearing to a later time. These rules identify the procedure the Court follows when it postpones a case and any changes needed to apply that procedure in the Supreme Court. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXIII. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "How are witnesses in attendance to be examined under this Rule?", "opts": ["Exclusively through sealed statements", "Only through pre-recorded evidence", "By written questionnaire in Chambers", "Orally in open Court"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The answer is Orally in open Court. The hearing brings the parties' cases and evidence before the Court. Giving evidence, recording it and obtaining the written record are different steps, each governed by the relevant procedural rule. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXIV Rule 2. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "In what format is shorthand evidence of attending witnesses to be taken?", "opts": ["Question and answer", "Chronological narrative only", "Affidavit paragraphs only", "Numbered legal submissions"], "ans": 0, "exp": "The answer is Question and answer. The hearing brings the parties' cases and evidence before the Court. Giving evidence, recording it and obtaining the written record are different steps, each governed by the relevant procedural rule. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXIV Rule 2. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "What is the status of the signed transcript of the shorthand note?", "opts": ["It is merely an unofficial aide-memoire", "It is deemed the witness’s deposition and forms part of the record", "It is returned to the witness and excluded from the record", "It substitutes for the final judgment"], "ans": 1, "exp": "The answer is It is deemed the witness’s deposition and forms part of the record. The hearing brings the parties' cases and evidence before the Court. Giving evidence, recording it and obtaining the written record are different steps, each governed by the relevant procedural rule. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXIV Rule 3. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "Who may obtain a certified copy of shorthand evidence upon payment of the prescribed fee?", "opts": ["Only a person nominated by the Registrar", "Any member of the public without restriction", "A party to the matter and the witness whose evidence was taken", "Only the advocate conducting cross-examination"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The answer is A party to the matter and the witness whose evidence was taken. The hearing brings the parties' cases and evidence before the Court. Giving evidence, recording it and obtaining the written record are different steps, each governed by the relevant procedural rule. The provision assigns this specific responsibility to the named person or body. Check the role against the exact action being asked about.", "source": "Order XXXIV Rule 4. Source: Gazette p. 39"},
    {"q": "Which rules of Code Order XXIII apply to withdrawal and adjustment of Supreme Court original suits?", "opts": ["Rules 2, 4 and 6", "Rules 1, 4 and 5", "Rules 3, 5 and 7", "Rules 1, 2 and 3"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The answer is Rules 1, 2 and 3. A party can seek to withdraw a lawsuit or resolve it by agreement. The terms attached to that step matter if the party later tries to bring another suit about the same subject. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXV Rule 1. Source: Gazette p. 40"},
    {"q": "Before filing a new suit on the same subject after permitted withdrawal, what must be done?", "opts": ["Comply with any terms or conditions imposed by the previous order", "Obtain permission from every witness in the prior suit", "Wait until an unrelated appeal is decided", "File an admission under Code Order XII"], "ans": 0, "exp": "The answer is Comply with any terms or conditions imposed by the previous order. A party can seek to withdraw a lawsuit or resolve it by agreement. The terms attached to that step matter if the party later tries to bring another suit about the same subject. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXV Rule 2. Source: Gazette p. 40"},
    {"q": "Which Code Order applies to payment into Court in Supreme Court original suits?", "opts": ["Order XXV of the First Schedule", "Order XXIV of the First Schedule", "Order XXII of the First Schedule", "Order XXVI of the First Schedule"], "ans": 1, "exp": "The answer is Order XXIV of the First Schedule. Payment into Court means depositing money through the Court in connection with a lawsuit. It is a procedural step in the dispute and is different from paying the fee charged to file a case. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXVI. Source: Gazette p. 40"},
    {"q": "Which rules of Code Order XXXVI are applied to procedure by way of special case?", "opts": ["Rules 1, 3 and 6 without exceptions", "Rules 2, 4 and 7 without exceptions", "Rules 1, 2 and 5, subject to stated textual exceptions", "Rules 3, 5 and 8, subject to stated exceptions"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The answer is Rules 1, 2 and 5, subject to stated textual exceptions. A special case puts an agreed question before the Court for a decision. The process has its own requirements for presenting the issue and is distinct from taking a normal appeal against a judgment. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXVII. Source: Gazette p. 40"},
    {"q": "From which sub-rule of Code Order XXXVI is the phrase about a decree following judgment excepted?", "opts": ["Rule 2(1)", "Rule 3(1)", "Rule 4(2)", "Rule 5(2)"], "ans": 3, "exp": "The answer is Rule 5(2). A special case puts an agreed question before the Court for a decision. The process has its own requirements for presenting the issue and is distinct from taking a normal appeal against a judgment. Apply this answer to the situation asked about, keeping any conditions in the question together with the rule.", "source": "Order XXXVII. Source: Gazette p. 40"},
    {"q": "How must a plaintiff identify other documents relied on as evidence in support of the claim?", "opts": ["By mentioning them only at trial", "By entering them in a list annexed or added to the plaint", "By filing them only after the defendant appears", "By submitting them solely to the Court Accountant"], "ans": 1, "exp": "The answer is By entering them in a list annexed or added to the plaint. A plaint is the document that starts a civil lawsuit and sets out what the claimant asks for. The rules distinguish what must be put in the plaint from what the Court does if it cannot accept the document.", "source": "Order XXVI Rule 10. Source: Gazette p. 37, Order XXVI Rule 10"},
    {"q": "Where should the plaintiff place the list of documents produced along with the plaint?", "opts": ["Only in the defendant’s written statement", "Only in the register of appeals", "Endorsed on or annexed to the plaint", "Only in an application for review"], "ans": 2, "exp": "The answer is Endorsed on or annexed to the plaint. A plaint is the document that starts a civil lawsuit and sets out what the claimant asks for. The rules distinguish what must be put in the plaint from what the Court does if it cannot accept the document. An annexure is an additional document attached to the main filing.", "source": "Order XXVI Rule 5. Source: Gazette p. 36, Order XXVI Rule 5"}
